RTP timestamps are derived from the source clock relative to a per-session
anchor, and a forward step larger than 500 ms is clamped. The source can
change under a running session, and the clamp then turns the jump into a
race that clients render as a stall:

- Audio: the mic is turned off and on again. The capture gap is real, but
  the clamp makes the audio clock lag real time; clients that sync to it
  freeze video for ~20 s and keep extra latency after every cycle.
- Video: a video restart recreates the encoder, whose timestamps start on
  a new base (about 20 s ahead on a T31). The clamp replays that jump at
  0.5 s per frame, so the video runs ahead of the audio and the client
  shows a frozen picture while the audio plays on.

Compare each jump with the monotonic time elapsed since the previous
packet. A jump that matches it is a real gap and is kept; one that does
not is absorbed into the session anchor, so the RTP clock keeps following
real time. Audio and video share the same helper.

